origin/v1/v114-wp1316-auto-inputs:docs/v1.1.4/FINDINGS.md:82:| A4 | P2 | Specialist contracts: `maxContextTokens` unused, `validateSpecialistOutput` has no runtime caller, required inputs are prompt advice, verdict gate covers reviewer units only | WP-1316 (new) | FIXED in WP-1316 (PRs #428 and the auto-inputs follow-up): ceiling at admission, required inputs enforced before inference, specialist results validated with raw rejected output kept, remedies (`set_inputs`, `replace_task`) that clear each block. The runner now collects `security_reviewer` (`unified_diff`, `authorized_scope`) and `failure_debugger` (`failure_log`, `reproduction_command`) inputs itself from operation and workspace state, under the `/team @file:` protected-path (read_file policy and case-insensitive `.nexus`) and redaction rules; the diff is built from changed paths (NUL-separated, rendering options pinned, protected paths and both sides of a rename removed, literal pathspecs), never by parsing diff text, through a scratch index (base tree plus intent-to-add entries for untracked files) so an unstaged move of a protected file is detected as a rename rather than shown as a new file, and the same builder (`candidateDiff`) serves `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` evidence; when any protected path changed, every added file is omitted too (a protected file moved past the similarity threshold, or copied out, could otherwise appear as a new file), and independently of git's heuristics an added file is also omitted when it equals a protected file present in the workspace (tracked, untracked or ignored) or shares a significant line (trimmed, non-comment, 8+ characters) with one, which covers `cp .env notes.txt`, a copy of an untouched tracked protected file (name-status runs with `--find-copies-harder`) and a partial copy past the similarity threshold (files in `.nexus` compare by exact content only; protected sources over 4 MiB or 64 MiB in total make every added file omitted); the stated limits, where credential redaction is the only guard, are lines pasted from a protected file into a file that already existed at the base (modified files are not line-compared), any re-formatting of a protected file's lines (quoting such as `"KEY=v",`, a changed separator such as `KEY: value`, values only, re-encoding: a line matches only when exactly equal once trimmed and at least 8 characters), and a protected file whose lines are all under 8 characters (caught only by an exact whole-file match); the trade-off is over-omission: a new file sharing a common line (such as `NODE_ENV=development`) with any protected file in the workspace, an ignored `.env*` included, is omitted and noted; the pass that lists ignored files has a 180 s timeout (about 6 s per 90k ignored files, so past roughly 2.5 million it fails closed with an error instead of being trusted); the protected-path policy is the operator's workspace policy, not the unit worktree's; an untracked nested git repository is skipped and counted; a diff over the output buffer is refused with a message to scope the inputs; the debugger snapshot is built with the same pinned git environment, the same stripped attribute source (so `text`/`eol` normalization matches the tester's own commit) and the same driver refusal; prerequisite summaries are rendered as JSON-encoded data; a changed auto-collected diff is sent again whole on resume (bounded by the input limit, by design, not as a delta); no repository-configured command runs while the diff is built (fsmonitor, hooks, replace objects, system attributes off; attributes come from a private tree of the worktree's own `.gitattributes` with every `filter` and `diff` assignment removed, so `text`/`eol` normalization still applies and no driver does, on git 2.40+ (older git ignores `GIT_ATTR_SOURCE` silently; see A4a); a filter or diff driver still assigned through `$GIT_DIR/info/attributes` fails closed); a unit whose verifier did not pass keeps that run on the unit (`failedRun`) and a dependent `failure_debugger` may run while the dependency is BLOCKED on it (the one exception to waiting for COMPLETE, recorded in the `operation.ts` lifecycle; a retried dependency is unfinished input again; that debugger runs on a snapshot of the failing candidate, bound to the failed run, and its agent skips the completion oracle entirely (`diagnosticRun`), so neither the configured verifier nor one it discovers after writing a file gates it); a resumed unit is given its current inputs; the `reproduction_command_source` label is derived only together with the command it describes; `authorized_scope` is the operation objective plus the unit's task, which is weaker than a declared file list, and a `replace_task` decision records the scope change (`scopeChange`); a `reproduction_command` is trusted only when operator-configured, otherwise labelled untrusted; caller `inputs` override; anything it cannot derive fails closed naming the input. Tests: `cli/test/specialist_contract_enforcement.test.ts`, `cli/test/specialist_auto_inputs.test.ts` (through `AgentWorkUnitExecutor.run` and `/team`). The verdict-gate clause is WITHDRAWN: `isReviewRole` already covers `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer`; `security_reviewer` has no verdict contract and gets the structured-result gate. |
origin/v1/v114-wp1316-auto-inputs:docs/v1.1.4/FINDINGS.md:83:| A4a (WP-1316 supported domain) | P2 | **OPEN, accepted limitation, for the owner at release review.** Three Entire review rounds each fixed one exotic git state and exposed the next, so per-state handling was replaced by one predicate, `snapshotSupport` (`cli/src/core/specialist_inputs.ts`), applied by `candidateDiff` and by the debugger snapshot (`snapshotCandidate`). **Supported:** a stage-0 index of files and symlinks (every `ls-files -v` tag `H`); no sparse checkout; `core.filemode` and `core.symlinks` true; `core.ignorecase` not true; no gitlink (submodule); no unresolved merge; a workspace at the repository root; SHA-1 objects; every path valid UTF-8; no `filter=` attribute and no `.gitattributes` over 1 MiB or unreadable. Exercised by the sweep in `cli/test/specialist_inputs_domain.test.ts` and not refused: detached HEAD, linked worktrees, untracked, ignored and nested-repository paths (in a plain checkout), intent-to-add entries, renames and copies, symlinks, a merge or cherry-pick resolved in the index. **Refused:** each state above that is not supported. **Checkout trust:** both builders run git only after `trustedGitArgs` (`verifyCheckout`) and against the pinned `--git-dir`/`--work-tree`, so a unit worktree whose `.git` pointer was replaced, or that holds a planted repository (a nested repository included), is refused and nothing of it is read or copied. **What each consumer sees on a refusal:** `security_reviewer` runs without the diff and is told why (`candidate diff unavailable: ...`); `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` fall back to a plain `git diff` against the base (`plainCandidateDiff`: private base-tree index, protected paths and protected content left out, no driver run, same checkout trust) and are told it is a plain diff, so a domain refusal does not block the review (an untrusted checkout, a driver refusal, a path count over 2,000, a timeout or any other git failure still blocks; the plain diff refuses (explicit error) when any path in the base tree, index or untracked listing is not valid UTF-8, even an unrelated one, and does not show a force-added ignored new file); a `failure_debugger` cannot be given a snapshot of a refused candidate, so the unit fails with the reason and an operator retry (fix the state, then retry) rather than debugging a wrong tree. A failed rebuild of a stale snapshot leaves the old worktree and branch in place. **Protected-content comparison (new files vs protected sources):** sound only for regular, readable files within the size bound; a protected path that is a symlink (never dereferenced), a special file, a directory in its place, oversized or unreadable means 'cannot compare' and is treated like a protected change: every new file is omitted and counted (`omittedNew`). While a readable protected source exists, every NEW symlink or other non-regular file is omitted too (a link to a directory is followed by `git diff --no-index`). `mv .env notes.txt` of an ignored, untracked `.env` is not detectable (the source is gone from disk and was never tracked): only credential redaction guards it. Protected sources are read through a no-follow descriptor whose real path is checked inside the repository on Linux (a parent directory swapped for a link after the check is refused); on other platforms that window is narrowed, not closed. Out of scope: encoded or re-formatted copies (base64, quoting, values only) and lines under 8 characters; hard links are regular files and are compared. Residual: lines pasted into a file that already existed (status M) are never line-compared; a commented-out secret line is not matched; in the plain diff for a subdirectory workspace the protected-source listings are relative to that subdirectory; `.nexus` links and files over the bound are left out by design; the snapshot does not run the comparison. The plain review diff keeps the real index's staged removals (`git rm --cached` of a file that stays on disk). **Known gaps, not claims of coverage:** the snapshot (`add -A`) and the diff leave out ignored files (`node_modules`, build output, `.env`), so a failure that depends on them is not reproduced; a `-diff` attribute can push the diff over its size limit (reported as too large); git older than 2.40 silently ignores `GIT_ATTR_SOURCE` (only `--path-format=absolute`, 2.31, errors), so a worktree `.gitattributes` driver would apply there and is caught only by the `filter=` refusal and `check-attr`; the predicate is evaluated once and the listings run after it, so a concurrent index change in a shared (non-isolated) checkout can slip between them; a partial clone is protected from lazy fetch only through `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH` (git 2.44+); an unreadable file or an old git fails closed with a raw git message. SHA-256 refusal is tested for the diff only. A finding outside this list is still possible. |

